INT. TAXIDERMY SHOP, BACK ROOM - NIGHT

Glass eyes in drawers. A stuffed owl on a shelf. A work lamp over a half-skinned fox on a steel table. Sawdust drifts like slow snow.

INSPECTOR CALEB ROURKE, 50s, tweed coat, notebook, turns a jar of amber eyeballs in his hands and sets it down. Across the table, MRS. OTTOLINE PRATT, 60s, gray bob, rubber apron, scrapes a pelt with a curved knife and does not look up.

ROURKE
(to the room)
Does anyone else work late here?

PRATT
Just me, Inspector.

ROURKE
(to himself)
Of course.

He walks the length of the shelves, peering into glass. A badger snarls. A heron stares. He stops at a stuffed spaniel and tilts his head.

ROURKE (CONT'D)
This is a fresh one.

PRATT
He belonged to the baker.

ROURKE
The baker died in March.

PRATT
He left instructions.

She lifts the pelt and shakes it. Sawdust hangs in the air. Rourke touches the spaniel's flank, then draws his fingers back and sniffs them. Pratt scrapes harder, the knife rasping, the fox on the table seeming to listen, and Rourke crosses to her side, leans on the table, plucks a glass eye from the tray, and holds it to the lamp.

ROURKE (CONT'D)
Mrs. Pratt, what is this?

PRATT
An eye.

ROURKE
It's a human eye.

PRATT
So it is.

She lays down the knife. The owl on the shelf shifts on its perch, just slightly, as if the floor had breathed.

ROURKE
(to the room)
Everyone, stay where you are.

PRATT
There's no everyone, Inspector.

ROURKE
(to himself)
That's what worries me.
